# Everything is LF unless a later rule says otherwise: git's text=auto
# heuristic leaves genuine binaries alone, and later byte-exact rules
# override. Retires the Windows-checkout CRLF-smudge class in one line.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that are executed or sourced by POSIX sh on Linux (including WSL
# builds from a Windows checkout) must stay LF: nginx's ./configure
# sources `config`/`filter/config`/`static/config` with sh, which treats
# a CR as part of the line and fails with "$'\r': command not found"
# when core.autocrlf=true has smudged the checkout to CRLF.
config text eol=lf
*/config text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
script/* text eol=lf
*.t text eol=lf
examples/*.conf text eol=lf

# Byte-exact fixtures: the static-module suite asserts Content-Length
# and serves .br frames verbatim — any EOL conversion corrupts them.
t/suite/* -text
fuzz/corpus/* -text
fuzz/fuzz.dict -text
# Any dictionary fixture is byte-significant -- libFuzzer token files
# (text-format but byte-exact) and RFC 9842 raw dictionaries alike.
# -text rather than binary keeps the libFuzzer ones diffable. .dat is
# the neutral extension Compression Dictionary Transport guidance
# recommends for web-served dictionaries; cover it for future examples.
*.dict -text
*.dat -text
*.br binary

# CI-only stub module for the vary-suppression witness; it claims a
# real third-party module's name and must never ship in archives.

# The CPAN dependency lock is byte-compared against cpanm output on a
# Linux runner: a CRLF smudge turns every line into a mismatch.
*.cpan.lock text eol=lf
